#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162991} :activity/id #uuid "d12cb1d7-31ed-5781-9246-28f5ab471279" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049728 :topic/id #uuid "0e84f145-35c9-580b-bb72-93e90f0414bb" :topic/math-academy-id 3052} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162991 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163282 :tutorial/content "We've already seen how to work with bivariate distributions for *discrete* random variables. This lesson will examine the case where the random variables are *continuous.* \n\nA function $f(x,y)$ is a **joint probability density function** (or **joint PDF**) for the two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ if the following three conditions are satisfied:\n\n- $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint_{\\mathbb R^2} f(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y = 1$\n\n- $P\\left((X,Y)\\in A\\right) = \\displaystyle \\iint_{A} f(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y,$ where $A$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\nThere is some nice intuition to these conditions.\n\n- The first condition states that $f$ must be nonnegative everywhere.\n\n- The second condition states that the sum (integral) of $f$ over possible values of $X$ and $Y$ must add up to $1.$\n\n- The third condition states that we compute the probability of an event $A$ by summing (integrating) $f$ over all regions associated with $A.$\n\nIt's worth taking a minute to compare these conditions with those we've seen previously. Firstly, they are the continuous analog of those for the joint PMF of two discrete random variables. Secondly, they are the two-dimensional analog of those for the PDF $f(x)$ of a (single) continuous random variable $X.$ \n\nIt's also worth noting that these conditions can be generalized to multivariate distributions $(X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n)$ of $n$ continuous random variables.\n\nLet's now take a look at a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "5efc289c-9713-5098-ba27-c3f5e5e11dc6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5472 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f6e94068-9978-5ab0-83cf-dac7f6f65311" :step/math-academy-id 34070 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163081}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162995 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162069 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161869 :question/id #uuid "7f92bdfc-9d59-585c-8917-9198c4a053f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-9183"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "129940fb-a409-55bc-9852-f68f4f9ffd11" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050264}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476977}] :knowledge-point/title "Joint PDFs for Continuous Random Variables: Non-Rectangular Domains"} :step/id #uuid "739504cf-ae0d-5786-974b-7de29bfd0f81" :step/math-academy-id 34255 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163077}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162996 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162070 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161870 :question/id #uuid "068714ca-5127-509c-a4e1-51374c6afbed" :question/math-academy-id "e-9184"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2bcf1eb2-cf04-5dd0-bb43-682c7e9cd953" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476905}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Using a Joint PDF"} :step/id #uuid "5052ef5c-00e3-5541-9f90-335c381fcb8f" :step/math-academy-id 34258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163001 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162075 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161875 :question/id #uuid "1ebb1eb5-e7f2-557c-aafa-e11dcb2b04e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9207"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a66952d-4e4b-5249-8f44-27d6fd198ea8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051001}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476959}] :knowledge-point/title "Joint PDFs for Continuous Random Variables: Rectangular Domains"} :step/id #uuid "8852dbbc-b2b7-500c-a044-cc3b72ccbd80" :step/math-academy-id 34353 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162995}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163329 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y.$ As previously discussed, for any subset $A$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have \n\n$$\nP\\big( (X,Y) \\in A \\big) = \\iint_A f(x, y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn particular, if $A$ is the event defined as\n\n$$\nA = \\big\\{ (X,Y) \\:|\\: a \\leq X \\leq b, \\, c \\leq Y \\leq d \\big\\},\n$$\n\nthen the corresponding probability can be computed as\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b, c \\leq Y \\leq d) = \\int^{d}_{c} \\int^{b}_{a} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y = \\int^{b}_{a} \\int^{d}_{c} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, analogously to the case of a single continuous random variable, the probability that $(X,Y)$ takes on any particular value $(x,y)$ is zero, i.e., \n\n$$\nP(X = x, Y=y) = 0.\n$$\n\nLet's see an example of how to compute a probability in practice." :tutorial/id #uuid "48e7fa44-96c7-5c63-a119-8ef92feda964" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6285 :tutorial/title "Finding Probabilities Using Joint PDFs"} :step/id #uuid "e264574e-0bf7-52ce-9591-11baee7b9188" :step/math-academy-id 38085 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162996}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163081 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163330 :tutorial/content "Let's show that the following function is a valid joint PDF:\n![](images/2f/2fcc03e45f23018485c2aff1ff3c6804899928e37bedaa05d9dafc5e02780179.svg)\n\nWe only need to check the first two conditions to show that this is a valid joint PDF for *some* pair of random variables. So let's do that:\n\n- Clearly $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ so the first condition is satisfied. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- To check the second condition, let's first define a region $D$ where $f(x)$ is nonzero:\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq   1,\\,0 \\leq y\\leq   1\\}\n$$\n\nA sketch of this region is shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/ea5ff670f03d0bffb84f0929d19b94bca62f23677f913c01e8e0949d144bc60a.png)\n\nLet's now integrate $f(x,y)$ over $\\mathbb R^2.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{x}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second condition is satisfied too.\n\nHence, $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function.\n\nWe'll learn how to compute probabilities using the joint PDF later in this lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "a5349a9d-3b66-5bdf-842d-bfd4b3e0660a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6289 :tutorial/title "Verifying That a Function Is a Valid Joint PDF"} :step/id #uuid "8086e474-064e-5f2e-9848-45ea654cd732" :step/math-academy-id 38099 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163001}}] :activity/title "Joint Distributions for Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162189}]}
